'''''Herbertus borealis''''' is a species of [[Marchantiophyta|liverwort]] in the family [[Herbertaceae]] known as '''northern prongwort'''.<ref name="BBS">{{cite book |editor=Ian Atherton |editor2=Sam Bosanquet |editor3=Mark Lawley |title=Mosses and Liverworts of Britain and Ireland: A Field Guide |publisher=[[British Bryological Society]] |year=2010 |isbn=978-0-9561310-1-0 |author=David Long |chapter=''Herbertus borealis'' |page=76}}</ref> It was described in 1970 by [[Alan Crundwell]].<ref name="Bell">{{cite journal |author=David Bell |author2=David Long |name-list-style=amp |year=2012 |title=European ''Herbertus'' and the 'Viking prongwort' |journal=[[Field Bryology]] |volume=106 |pages=3–14 |url=http://rbg-web2.rbge.org.uk/bbs/Activities/field%20bryology/FB106/FB106%20Bell%20Long.pdf }}</ref> It is [[endemism|endemic]] to Scotland, where it is found only in the [[Beinn Eighe]] nature reserve, and lives in [[dwarf shrub]] heath alongside other large liverworts such as ''[[Anastrophyllum donnianum]]'', ''[[Bazzania tricrenata]]'' and ''[[Pleurozia purpurea]]''.<ref name="BBS"/> A closely related species, described in 2012 as ''[[Herbertus norenus]]'' and known as "Viking prongwort", is known from [[Shetland]] and [[Norway]] and was formerly confused with ''H. borealis''.<ref name="Bell"/>

Following molecular studies and based on morphological grounds, it was synonymized with ''H. delavayi'', a species from Yunan, China.<ref>{{Cite journal|doi=10.1179/174328205X72496|title=On the identity of Herbertus borealis (Jungermanniopsida: Herbertaceae), with notes on the possible origin of H. sendtneri|year=2005|last1=Feldberg|first1=K.|last2=Heinrichs|first2=J.|journal=Journal of Bryology|volume=27|issue=4|pages=343-350}}</ref> However, after further study on key differences of DNA barcode sequences and key distinguishing features it was recommended these species remain distinct.<ref>{{Cite journal|doi=10.1111/j.1755-0998.2011.03053.x|title=DNA barcoding of European Herbertus (Marchantiopsida, Herbertaceae) and the discovery and description of a new species|year=2011|last1=Bell|first1=D.|last2=Long|first2=D.G.|last3=Forrest|first3=A.D.|last4=Hollingsworth|first4=M.L.|last5=Blom|first5=H.H.|last6=Hollingsworth|first6=P.M.|journal=Molecular Ecology Resources|volume=12|issue=1|pages=36-47}}</ref> These variations were later suggested to arise from environmental conditions, arguing for of ''H. Borealis'' to be further synonymized with multiple other members of the Herbertus family, including ''H. buchii'', ''H. dicranus'', ''H. kurzii'', ''H. longifissus'', ''H. norenus'', and ''H. stramineus'' under the broader ''H. aduncus''.<ref>{{Cite journal|doi=10.1080/14772000.2017.1291542|title=Contrasting patterns of postglacial range shifts between the northern and southern hemisphere in Herbertus (Herbertaceae, Marchantiophyta)|year=2017|last1=He|first1=S.|last2=Sun|first2=Y.|journal=Systematics and Biodiversity|volume=15|issue=6|pages=541-551}}</ref> This was later challenged by the claim that they often grow in close proximity, adding that it does not account for the clear morphological differences that arise from the small genetic variations, and has been subsequently rejected by some.<ref>{{Cite journal|doi=10.1080/03736687.2019.1694329|title=An annotated checklist of bryophytes of Europe, Macaronesia and Cyprus|year=2020|last1=Hodgetts|first1=N.G.|last2=Söderström|first2=L.|last3=Blockeel|first3=T.L.|last4=Caspari|display-authors=etal|journal=Journal of Bryology|volume=42|issue=1|pages=1-116|hdl=10138/318856|hdl-access=free}}</ref> More research is therefore proposed as this debate remains unresolved.

